Ladybird’s scripting.executeScript() waits for each document to be
idle unless the call asks to inject immediately. So do Chromium,
whose ScriptingExecuteScriptFunction hands inject_immediately to
its ScriptExecutor; Gecko, whose ext-scripting.js sets runAt to
document_idle unless injectImmediately is set; and WebKit, whose
injectionTime is DocumentIdle by default.

Results and errors follow Chromium’s ScriptingExecuteScriptFunction::
OnScriptExecuted(): With one frame, the script’s error rejects the call;
with more, frames that failed are left out, and the top frame comes
first. Ladybird keeps Chromium’s exact texts where extensions compare
them: “Cannot specify 'allFrames' if either 'frameIds' or 'documentIds'
is specified.” from its scripting_utils.cc (Gecko says “Cannot
specify both 'allFrames' and 'frameIds'.”), “No frame with id N in tab
with id M”, “Frame with ID N was removed.” from its ScriptExecutor,
and the host-access errors.

activeTab reaches only frames of the tab’s own origin in Ladybird, and
never a browser page, which is how Chromium’s activeTab grant (a granted
origin, checked frame by frame) behaves; and a refused child frame’s URL
appears in the error only with the tabs permission, as in Chromium.
